      "text": "To find the smallest positive two-digit multiple of \\(3\\), we need to identify the smallest two-digit number that is divisible by \\(3\\). The smallest two-digit number is \\(10\\), and we check if \\(10\\) is divisible by \\(3\\). Since \\(10 \\div 3 = 3\\) with a remainder of \\(1\\), \\(10\\) is not divisible by \\(3\\). The next number is \\(11\\), and checking \\(11 \\div 3 = 3\\) with a remainder of \\(2\\), \\(11\\) is also not divisible by \\(3\\). The next number is \\(12\\), and checking \\(12 \\div 3 = 4\\) with no remainder, \\(12\\) is divisible by \\(3\\). Therefore, the smallest positive two-digit multiple of \\(3\\) is \\(12\\). So, \\(a = 12\\).\n\nNext, to find the smallest positive three-digit multiple of \\(4\\), we need to identify the smallest three-digit number that is divisible by \\(4\\). The smallest three-digit number is \\(100\\), and we check if \\(100\\) is divisible by \\(4\\). Since \\(100 \\div 4 = 25\\) with no remainder, \\(100\\) is divisible by \\(4\\). Therefore, the smallest positive three-digit multiple of \\(4\\) is \\(100\\). So, \\(b = 100\\).\n\nNow, we need to find the sum \\(a + b\\):\n\\[\na + b = 12 + 100 = 112\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{112}\n\\]",
